course: dictionary
lesson: 3703
title: Flavour and its many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Flavour is a good example. It can mean the taste of food, a kind of taste, the act of adding taste, or a special feeling. «Ice cream comes in many flavours» uses flavours to mean kinds of taste, because ice cream is not sold in many feelings.
explain: Try the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. In «The festival had a Mexican flavour», the word does not mean taste at all. It means the festival had the feel and character of Mexico, with its music, food and colours.
explain: Flavour is a word that works in many subjects. In science, flavour comes from taste and smell working together. In maths, we use favourite flavours to make tally charts and bar graphs. In everyday life, you see flavours on crisp packets, juice bottles and ice cream tubs.
fact: Flavour means the taste of food or drink, as in «a lemon flavour».
fact: A flavour is one kind of taste a food is made in, as in «ice cream comes in many flavours».
fact: To flavour means to add something to food to give it a taste, as in «flavour the soup with herbs».
fact: Flavour can also mean a special feeling or character, as in «the festival had a Mexican flavour».
fact: Your tongue can detect sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami, but smell adds much more: «Most of the flavour of food comes from smell.»
exercise: «Most of the flavour of food comes from smell.» What does flavour mean in this sentence? | *The taste of food | A bag of crisps | A loud noise from the kitchen | A tall, thin kind of tree
exercise: «Ice cream comes in many flavours.» What does flavours mean here? | *Kinds of taste a food can be made in | Sounds made by spoons | Cold places in a freezer | Sizes of cones
exercise: Mum says, «I will flavour the soup with herbs.» What will she do? | To count the bowls | To cool the soup down | To pour the soup away | *To add herbs to give the soup a taste
test: Our class voted for our favourite ice-cream flavours. Which kind of chart could show the votes? | A map of the school | A recipe for soup | *A tally chart or bar graph | A poem about summer
test: «Many medicines are naturally bitter, so makers add sweet flavours for children.» Why do makers add sweet flavours? | To make the medicine colder | To stop the medicine being sold | To make the medicine stronger | *To make the medicine taste nicer for children
exam: «When a cold blocks your nose, your dinner tastes of almost nothing.» Why does this happen? | The dinner has gone off | Your tongue has fallen asleep | The dinner is too cold to taste | *Much of flavour is really smell, and a blocked nose means less smell
